## 3. How to start, from zero

Four stages. Each one is testable on its own — do them in order, because each proves the
assumptions the next one rests on.

### Stage 1 — Prove the node on the bench (no scooter)

1. Install the Arduino IDE, add the **arduino-esp32 core v3.x** board package, and the
   **NimBLE-Arduino** (v1.4.x), **OneWire** and **DallasTemperature** libraries.
2. Select the ESP32-C6 board, flash
   [firmware/warivo-node/warivo-node.ino](firmware/warivo-node/warivo-node.ino) over USB-C.
3. Power the board from USB and open the serial monitor at 115200. You want
   `BLE advertising as Warivo-Node`.
4. Install **nRF Connect** on any phone. Scan, connect to `Warivo-Node`, subscribe to
   `fff1`, and watch JSON frames arrive ~5×/sec. Voltage and speed will read zero — no
   sensors are attached yet. That is a pass.

If the frames look truncated in nRF Connect, that is the MTU: see §5.

### Stage 2 — Wire the scooter

⚠️ **60 V packs push real current.** Split R1 across two resistors so no single part sees
the full pack, fuse the divider tap, insulate every high-voltage joint, and **tie the
ESP32 ground to battery negative** — without a common ground every reading is garbage.

1. **Voltage** → `GPIO1`. Divider R1 = 270 kΩ (as 2× 135 kΩ in series), R2 = 10 kΩ,
   ratio 28. Add the 3.3 V zener + 100 nF cap at the tap.
2. **Speed** → `GPIO10`. A3144 hall + wheel magnet, 10 kΩ pull-up.
3. **Current** → `GPIO2`. ACS758 inline on the main cable. Set `USE_CURRENT = true`.
4. **Temperature** → `GPIO11`. DS18B20 with a 4.7 kΩ pull-up. Index 0 = ambient,
   index 1 = pack.
5. Calibrate: compare the reported voltage against a multimeter and adjust
   `DIVIDER_RATIO`. Roll the wheel one full turn and check the odometer moves by your
   wheel circumference.

### Stage 3 — Build the launcher

No JDK or Android SDK is required on your machine if you use Android Studio, which brings
both.

```bash
# Android Studio: File → Open → android/warivo-launcher → Sync → Run
# or, from the command line, with JDK 17 + the Android SDK already installed:
cd android/warivo-launcher
cp local.properties.example local.properties   # then edit sdk.dir
gradle wrapper                                 # one-time: creates ./gradlew
./gradlew assembleDebug
adb install -r app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk
```

**This has never been compiled.** Expect to fix a dependency version or two on the first
sync — [android/warivo-launcher/README.md](android/warivo-launcher/README.md) has a
troubleshooting table for the four likely failures (AGP vs Studio version, the MapLibre
version pin, the Kotlin/Compose-plugin pairing, and icon names). Those are version
archaeology, not logic bugs.

Install it and open it **as a normal app first**. Without Device Owner it is ordinary and
exitable, which is the right way to develop. Grant the location permission when asked —
Android ties **BLE discovery** to location permission, so without it the scan silently
finds nothing and the dashboard sits on "Looking for Warivo-Node…" forever.

### Stage 4 — Lock it down (do this last)

Device Owner can only be granted on a **factory-reset phone with no accounts added**.

```bash
# after factory reset: skip account setup, enable developer options + USB debugging
adb install -r app-debug.apk
adb shell dpm set-device-owner com.warivo.os/.kiosk.AdminReceiver
```

Then in **Setup**, turn on *Lock to the dashboard on boot*. From then on the phone boots
straight into the dashboard: no status bar, no nav bar, no recents, no way out.

**Know the way back before you do this.** Setup has two escapes: *Unlock now* drops Lock
Task for the session, *Release phone* undoes every policy and gives up Device Owner for
good (it cannot be re-granted without another factory reset). Test a build unlocked before
you lock it.

