## 4. The UI

Landscape, dark, laid out like a car head unit rather than a phone app: near-black page,
rounded cards with hairline edges, and a **bottom dock of five unlabelled monochrome
icons**. The dock is at the bottom because that is the edge a thumb reaches on a
bar-mounted phone, and it is the same five targets in the same places every time, so it
needs no aim at speed.

- **Drive** — three-column card grid. Speed gets the big tick-marked ring gauge because it
  is the only value read while moving; battery gets a second ring; everything else
  (power, current, odometer, consumption, temps) is a small card, because those are
  checked at a stop. Gear, throttle and the indicator/headlight/high-beam/parking/reverse
  lamps appear by themselves once the node sends them.
- **Map** — MapLibre with raster OpenStreetMap, following the phone's GPS. Areas you have
  ridden through stay available offline via MapLibre's disk cache.
- **Music** — artwork and big round transport controls on one side, the library list on the
  other, so changing track never hides what is playing. Output routes to the paired
  Bluetooth speaker over A2DP automatically.
- **Search** — a Google-only WebView behind a pill field with a round accent button.
  Off-Google hosts are refused, so a tapped result can't turn the head unit into a browser.
- **Setup** — link state, proximity beep (off by default), kiosk toggle, escape hatch.

Panels do **not** swipe. The map and the search WebView both consume horizontal drags, so
a pager would fight them on exactly the two panels where it matters.

